# A data/AI engineer's portfolio playbook for 2026

**The strongest 2026 data/AI portfolios do three things:** they ship a RAG chatbot grounded on the owner's real projects, embed one signature interactive artifact (an animated ETL pipeline or live CVE/ML demo), and front-load metric-led case studies that a recruiter can scan in ten seconds. Everything else — the templates, the 3D heroes, the skill radars — is table-stakes polish. Your stack (Data Eng + AI + Cyber + React fluency + TransLink/Wisr credibility) is almost perfectly aligned with what differentiates at the top end. Below is a concrete build plan: where to host, what to build, which libraries, and which portfolios to steal ideas from.

## Hosting: Vercel for speed, Cloudflare Pages for "truly free"

Pick based on one question — **will your portfolio run an AI chatbot backend?** If yes, you need serverless functions, which kills GitHub Pages on its own.

| Platform | Best use | Killer feature | Deal-breaker |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Vercel Hobby** | Next.js + AI chatbot, fast DX | Native Next.js App Router, `ai` SDK, Edge Functions, 1M invocations/mo | Hobby plan formally **prohibits commercial use**; jumps to $20/seat if you monetize |
| **Cloudflare Pages + Workers** | Free forever, no commercial restrictions | **Unlimited bandwidth**, ~0ms edge cold start, Workers AI + Vectorize built-in for RAG | Slightly rougher Next.js support (needs `@cloudflare/next-on-pages`); fewer DX niceties |
| **GitHub Pages** | Pure static, zero config, `yourname.github.io` branding | Free forever, free HTTPS, custom domain | Static only — **cannot host the chatbot backend**; SPA routing gotcha (404 on refresh) |
| **Netlify** | Forms + plugins | Free Netlify Forms (re-freed April 2026) | Credit-based free tier (300 credits/mo ≈ 20 builds) is a downgrade; sites pause when out |

**Recommendation:** Deploy the static frontend to Vercel (best DX) or Cloudflare Pages (best free tier), and host the chatbot endpoint as a Route Handler (Vercel) or Worker (Cloudflare). If you want the `.github.io` URL for the credibility signal, front it with a custom subdomain and call an external Worker for the AI API — this gives you GitHub Pages branding without losing server-side features.

## The three signature features that will place you in the top 1%

Most data-engineer portfolios fail on the same thing: they list tools instead of demonstrating them. These three features turn the portfolio itself into proof of work, and every one is achievable on free tier.

**1. "Ask my career anything" — a RAG chatbot grounded in your resume and projects.** This is the single highest-leverage feature for an AI engineer portfolio in 2026. The clearest production example is **Cameron Rye's "Ask"** at rye.dev — built on Cloudflare Workers AI + Vectorize + Llama 3.3 + BGE embeddings, streams under 200ms, and crucially frames itself as a *third-person expert system about his work*, not a clone of him (which avoids the uncanny-valley problem). Implementation stack: Vercel AI SDK `useChat` hook on the frontend, **Groq's free `llama-3.3-70b-versatile`** or Gemini 2.0 Flash free tier for inference, **Upstash Vector** or Supabase pgvector for the store, OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` or Gemini `text-embedding-004` for embeddings. Rye's full writeup is a ready-made blueprint.

**2. An interactive React Flow diagram of a real pipeline you built.** Take your TransLink GTFS ETL or Wisr threat-intel ingestion and render it as a clickable, zoomable node graph using **@xyflow/react** (36k stars, the dominant node-UI library). Each node = an Azure service with the official Azure icon; click reveals row counts, latency, code snippet, or a mini architecture explainer. For the "data packet flowing through the edge" effect, use the `AnimatedNodeEdge` pattern with CSS `offset-path` rather than React Flow's built-in `animated: true` (which tanks CPU past 50 edges). Supplement with **Mermaid.js** diagrams committed to your MDX project pages — they're version-controlled and GitHub renders them natively.

**3. A live Streamlit or Gradio demo embedded on a project page.** Host on **Hugging Face Spaces** (free CPU tier) using the `<gradio-app>` web component, which is faster than an iframe. On-brand demos for your profile: a **CVE-to-CVSS-vector classifier** (pastes a vulnerability description, predicts CVSS base score + reasoning), a **transit demand forecaster** on GTFS data, or a **log anomaly detector** that flags suspicious lines. One live demo beats ten screenshots; a recruiter can click, *try it*, and immediately have something concrete to tell the hiring manager.

## Section-by-section blueprint with data-specific twists

The structural rule is **layered disclosure in a single page**: satisfy the recruiter's 10-second scan in the top 600px, reward the hiring manager's 2-3 minute dig below. Research from InterviewPal (2025) puts recruiter first-pass at 11.2 seconds and median review at 1 minute 34 seconds — design for both without a cringe-worthy "recruiter mode" toggle.

**Hero (the 10-second test).** Your name as H1, a sharp role line (*"Data & AI Engineer — I ship Azure lakehouses and RAG systems for security-critical data"* beats any passion statement), three credibility chips (*MS Data Analytics Eng • Ex-TransLink, Wisr AI • Vancouver/Remote*), primary CTAs to Projects and Resume PDF, and a muted strip of prior-employer logos. If you want a signature visual, a **React Three Fiber** particle cloud drifting through a tube (data-lake metaphor) or a rotating embedding space is on-brand for AI engineers without being gimmicky — just lazy-load it behind `React.lazy` because R3F adds ~200KB gzipped. **Magic UI's `AnimatedBeam` and `OrbitingCircles`** are a lighter alternative and pair Azure, Spark, LangChain logos into an animated constellation.

**Impact strip.** One row, three to four numbers: *"2B+ transit events processed/day · $180K/yr Azure cost optimized · 12 RAG features in prod · 4 technical articles."* This single row does more work than a skills section — it's the recruiter's entire decision signal.

**Featured projects (three, tag-filterable).** Research from The Data Forge (April 2026) is unambiguous: **if a project doesn't have an architecture diagram, it doesn't exist to a reviewer.** Each card needs a one-line problem statement, tech pills, a quantified impact metric, and a reading-time estimate. Behind each card, a project page follows the consensus format: *Brief Overview box → Context → Approach with rejected alternatives → Architecture diagram → What broke and how you handled it → Scale/cost → Impact → Learnings*. The "what broke" section is the single strongest senior-signal — it separates tutorial-copiers from engineers who've shipped.

**Skills — treat them as data, not decoration.** A **Recharts `RadarChart`** of your category proficiencies (Data Eng, ML/AI, Cloud, Security, Frontend) is the honest version of the skills section. Pair it with a **visx `HeatmapRect`** where rows are skills, columns are years, and color intensity shows usage — this is a uniquely data-engineer-flavored take on the usual skill cloud. Avoid progress bars ("I'm 87% at Python" is nobody's idea of credible). Borrow Caio Furlan's **D3 tech-radar** implementation from `caiokf/dev-profile` if you want the ThoughtWorks-style Adopt/Trial/Assess/Hold rings.

**Experience timeline.** Use **react-chrono** or a custom vertical timeline with GSAP ScrollTrigger pinning. Each role card should expand on hover to reveal tech stack plus a miniature architecture diagram — TransLink card surfaces Azure Data Factory + Synapse + 4M daily smart-card taps; Wisr card surfaces LangChain RAG over 200+ CVE feeds. Company logos as recognizable anchors.

**Live GitHub integration.** Combine **react-github-calendar** (contribution heatmap), **anuraghazra/github-readme-stats** (language donut), and **skillicons.dev** for the tech strip. For the wow moment, port the **3D contribution skyline** from the napjose.ph tutorial into React Three Fiber — it's the GitHub Skyline aesthetic rendered in-browser.

**Contact.** A terminal-style form (references: `satnaing/terminal-portfolio` with six themes, or `aisurf3r/terminal-portfolio` with autocomplete) is a well-worn pattern that still lands well for technical audiences. Include a Calendly embed, an email address, and GitHub/LinkedIn.

## The 2026 visualization and animation stack

| Purpose | Library | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Node/edge diagrams, pipelines | **@xyflow/react** (React Flow) | 36k stars; the standard. Animate edges with `offset-path` not `animated:true` |
| Text-based diagrams in MDX | **Mermaid.js** | 76k stars; `architecture-beta` syntax now supports 200k+ Iconify icons including Azure |
| Charts / KPIs | **Recharts** + **Tremor** | Recharts for radars/bars; Tremor (tremor.so) gives dashboard-grade KPI tiles out of the box |
| Low-level custom viz | **visx** (Airbnb) or **Nivo** | visx for heatmaps/xycharts; Nivo for prettier defaults and SSR |
| 3D hero moments | **@react-three/fiber** + **drei** | Declarative Three.js; code-split it (heavy bundle) |
| Large geospatial data | **deck.gl** / **kepler.gl** | Millions of points on WebGL — perfect for a TransLink ridership heatmap over Vancouver |
| Motion/scroll | **Framer Motion (Motion)** + **GSAP ScrollTrigger** | Motion for declarative; GSAP for scroll-choreographed cinematic sequences |
| Flashy UI accents | **Magic UI**, **Aceternity UI**, **React Bits** | Copy-paste animated components (NumberTicker, Spotlight, AnimatedBeam, OrbitingCircles) |
| Foundation | **shadcn/ui + Tailwind v4 + Next.js 15** | The 2026 default; owns the code, fully customizable |

**For your cybersecurity angle specifically:** a **MITRE ATT&CK matrix** rendered from the official STIX JSON (available at `mitre/cti`) with cells colored by techniques you've built detections for — implementable as a CSS grid with React on top, no specialized library needed. Pair with an **EPSS × CVSS scatter plot** (Nivo ScatterPlot: x=severity, y=exploit probability, bubble=age) pulling live from the NVD and FIRST APIs. Reference dashboards worth studying: `viswsd/cve-threat-dashboard` (forkable, uses Chart.js radar for CVSS breakdown) and `cve.icu` (polished public design). For threat-intel graphs, **Cytoscape.js** handles STIX relationships better than React Flow at scale.

## Creative AI integrations beyond the chatbot

The RAG chatbot is the headline, but 2026 has opened up additional patterns worth stealing. **Persona-aware content** — a small toggle that reruns your project summaries through an LLM tuned to "recruiter" vs "engineer" vs "curious visitor" tone — directly showcases prompt engineering. **Semantic project search** using **Transformers.js** lets you run MiniLM embeddings entirely in the browser (zero backend cost) so visitors can query "projects with Azure + streaming + cost optimization" and get ranked results. **A visible multi-agent demo** using LangGraph or CrewAI, with the agent graph rendered live in React Flow as they execute, directly dogfoods your Wisr AI experience. The most gimmicky but memorable option is **voice navigation** via Web Speech API + LLM function calling ("show me your cybersecurity projects" scrolls/filters the page) — niche but people tweet about it.

**Hosting the AI backend for free** is a solved problem in 2026: **Groq** (free tier, sub-second 70B inference), **Google Gemini 2.0 Flash** (generous free tier), **Cloudflare Workers AI** (edge-deployed, fits the no-cost Cloudflare Pages story), and **Upstash Vector** for the store. Vercel's AI template gallery (`vercel.com/templates/ai`) has drop-in Groq and Gemini chatbot starters you can fork in minutes.

