## What will hurt you — avoid these patterns

Research from KDnuggets (September 2025), The Data Forge (April 2026), and DataExpert.io converges on the same failure modes, and most data portfolios hit at least three of them. **Tutorial copies with the serial numbers filed off** — Titanic, Iris, verbatim Data Engineering Zoomcamp pipelines — are instantly recognizable to any reviewer and signal you haven't shipped anything of your own. **Notebooks without business context** ("here's a model with 94% accuracy") fail the "so what?" test. **Tool obsession** — 30 skill bubbles with progress bars — signals desperation, not breadth; pick 5-7 you'd happily be interviewed on. **No architecture diagram** on a project page makes it invisible to technical reviewers. **No failure-mode discussion** ("when the API rate-limited us we…") makes projects feel academic. **Fake testimonials** ("Amazing engineer! — Sarah, PM") are worse than none. **Dead GitHub repos** with no README and a last-commit date two years out undo the narrative your portfolio is building.

On performance: **don't lazy-load above-the-fold content** — web.dev data shows lazy-loaded LCP elements actually worsen Core Web Vitals (median LCP 3,546ms vs 2,922ms). Hero images, hero charts, the first project card should be eager. Lazy-load heavy PowerBI embeds, R3F canvases, and live iframes behind IntersectionObserver or `React.lazy`. Keep the hero route JS bundle under 150KB gzipped and aim for LCP under 1.5 seconds — recruiters on office wifi bail on slow sites.

## SEO: own your name on Google in four weeks

Set **title tag** to `"[Your Name] — Data & AI Engineer | Azure, RAG, ETL"` (full name + specialty). Add **JSON-LD `Person` schema** in the root layout so Google recognizes you as an entity. Generate **per-project Open Graph images** with `@vercel/og` — proper OG images lift link engagement 38–291% (RankingBySEO data) and matter enormously when recruiters share your link in Slack. Submit the sitemap to Google Search Console and request indexing. Link to the site from your LinkedIn "Website" field, GitHub profile README, and Medium/Dev.to bios. Write two or three technical articles on Medium linking back — LinkedIn *articles* (not posts) also get indexed and carry meaningful link authority. Within four weeks `yourname.io` typically outranks LinkedIn for your name.

## Bottom line: a concrete build order

For your specific profile, the highest-leverage one-month build is:

**Week 1** — Fork `dillionverma/portfolio`, wire up Next.js 15 + Tailwind + shadcn/ui on Vercel. Write three project case studies (one Azure ETL flagship from TransLink, one RAG/LLM flagship from Wisr, one cybersecurity-specific CVE/CVSS demo) with mandatory architecture diagrams in Mermaid. Add the impact strip, logos, and hero.

**Week 2** — Build the React Flow interactive pipeline diagram for the TransLink project. Add Recharts radar and visx heatmap for skills. Integrate `react-github-calendar` + `github-readme-stats`.

**Week 3** — Ship the RAG chatbot: Vercel AI SDK + Groq free tier + Upstash Vector, grounded on your resume PDF and project write-ups, with Cameron Rye's "third-person expert system" prompt pattern. Host a Gradio CVE classifier on Hugging Face Spaces and embed it via `<gradio-app>`.

**Week 4** — OG images, JSON-LD schema, Google Search Console, Plausible or Vercel Analytics, write one Medium article on building the chatbot (which doubles as backlinks and a portfolio artifact itself).

The result — minimal aesthetic, bold data-viz accents, one signature interactive artifact per technical domain, and a working LLM feature that *demonstrates* the RAG/vector/LangChain skills instead of just listing them — will sit comfortably in the top 1% of data/AI engineer portfolios in 2026, while running entirely on free tiers.